From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 09:16:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Run each database's work on its one connection, in
 order

The engine had two kinds of connection per database: one for the
manager thread's metadata calls (versions, store and index metadata, the
restores after an abort), each in a storage transaction of its own, and
a new one for each batch of requests, opened on the thread pool (SQLite
opened a connection and ran its pragmas each time; RDB shared the store
handle). The next change keeps one storage transaction open across the
batches of an IndexedDB transaction, and a second connection can't work
alongside that: a metadata write would wait out the busy timeout behind
the open transaction (5 s on SQLite, 2 s on RDB, where begin also holds
the store lock) and fail, stalling the manager thread of every origin.

Each database now has an actor that owns its only connection and runs
everything on it one job at a time, in the order the manager hands it
over: the batches and the metadata calls. It is a job queue on the
global thread pool rather than a thread: a pool task runs the jobs while
any wait and then returns, so an idle database holds no thread. A
WPT process keeps every database it opened until it is deleted, so
threads per database would add up. The executors are Send, and RDB has
no thread affinity for its connections. The manager's metadata calls
wait for their result; when the actor is idle they run right away on
the manager thread, without a round trip through the pool. Dropping the
engine closes the connection after the queued jobs, so the manager can
delete the files right after.

Gone: SqlEngine's manager-thread connection and its RefCell, the
per-batch connections, the connector, the read and write pools and the
batch queue of the earlier change, whose job the actor's queue does.
SqlExecutor::connector stays for the executor tests that look at a
database from a second connection, and only for them.

The batches keep their semantics: each runs in one storage transaction,
readwrite replies wait for COMMIT, readonly ones go out as they run, and
the metadata calls still each run in a transaction of their own.

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits`: 83 passed, as before. DAYU200 `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage -- [--no-default-features] --features ohos-rdb-backend --status-level all`: RDB only 84 passed, both backends 138 passed, 2 skipped each (the documented test_new_storage_threads_create_independent_groups and the ignored long test), as before. `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets` warning-free with SQLite only, RDB only and both. Host WPT IndexedDB/ with SpiderMonkey at this commit: 3,224 of 4,511 subtests passed before, 3,226 now with no unexpected result (the two more are whole-test timeouts of the earlier run); idbtransaction-oncomplete, window and worker, `--repeat 30 --processes 4`: 60 of 60.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent designed the actor, moved the engine onto it and validated it on the host and the DAYU200)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
